/* ===== HARD questions (E1): Orlando Test 3 replica + circuit puzzles ===== */
MC("c03", 2, "Which law is stated by the relation E = IR, where E is the voltage across a conductor, I the current through it and R its resistance?", ["Coulomb’s law","Ohm’s law","Faraday’s law","Ampère’s law"], 1, "V = IR is Ohm’s law. Coulomb’s law is about the force between charges, Faraday’s about induced EMF, Ampère’s about the field made by currents.", {"dg":1});
MC("c07", 2, "Kirchhoff’s current law (the sum of currents into a node is zero) is an application of which conservation law?", ["Conservation of energy","Conservation of momentum","Conservation of charge","Conservation of flux"], 2, "Charge cannot pile up at a node in steady state, so current in = current out: KCL is conservation of charge. KVL (loop rule) is conservation of energy.", {"dg":1});
MC("c13", 2, "Which law is the fundamental operating principle of transformers, inductors, and many electric motors, generators and solenoids?", ["Faraday’s law of induction","Ohm’s law","Kirchhoff’s second law","Coulomb’s law"], 0, "A changing magnetic flux induces an EMF (ε = −N dΦ/dt). That is Faraday’s law; it underlies every device that couples a changing magnetic field to a coil.", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 3, "Who realized that the leg of a frog, in Galvani’s twitching-leg experiments, acted as an electrolyte (a moist conductor between two metals) and so built the first battery from that insight?", ["Charles-Augustin de Coulomb","Georg Ohm","André-Marie Ampère","Alessandro Volta"], 3, "Volta argued the twitching came from the two dissimilar metals and the moist tissue acting as an electrolyte, not from “animal electricity”. This led to the voltaic pile (1800).", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 2, "Which scientist discovered the quantitative relationship (an inverse-square law) between electrostatic charges?", ["Michael Faraday","Charles-Augustin de Coulomb","Gustav Kirchhoff","Nikola Tesla"], 1, "Coulomb measured the force between charges with a torsion balance (1785): F = k q₁q₂ / r².", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 3, "Who theorized the existence of an “electrodynamic molecule” (tiny circulating currents inside magnetic materials)?", ["Georg Ohm","Alessandro Volta","André-Marie Ampère","Gustav Kirchhoff"], 2, "Ampère explained magnetism as tiny circulating currents in matter, an idea later confirmed by the electron-orbit and spin picture.", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 3, "Georg Ohm gave his complete theory of electricity in a book whose title begins Die galvanische Kette, mathematisch bearbeitet. In what year did it appear, and what key relation did it contain?", ["1827; the current is proportional to the applied voltage (V ∝ I)","1785; force between charges falls as 1/r²","1831; induced EMF is proportional to the rate of flux change","1800; current is proportional to the number of cells only"], 0, "Ohm published in 1827 and showed that the potential difference across a conductor is directly proportional to the current through it, V = IR.", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 3, "Who invented electromagnetic rotary devices (1821) that became the foundation of electric-motor technology?", ["Hippolyte Pixii","Michael Faraday","Georg Ohm","Charles Coulomb"], 1, "In 1821 Faraday demonstrated continuous electromagnetic rotation (a wire circling a magnet), the first motor principle.", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 3, "Who built the first electric motor with a commutator that was powerful enough to turn machinery (1832)?", ["Michael Faraday","Hippolyte Pixii","William Sturgeon","André-Marie Ampère"], 2, "William Sturgeon (1832) built the first commutator-type DC motor able to drive machinery. Faraday’s 1821 device only rotated a wire around a magnet.", {"dg":1});
MC("c17", 3, "Who built the first hand-cranked dynamo (generator) using a commutator in 1832, at Ampère’s suggestion?", ["William Sturgeon","Moritz von Jacobi","Michael Faraday","Hippolyte Pixii"], 3, "Pixii’s dynamo (1832) used a rotating magnet and a commutator suggested by Ampère to turn the alternating output into a one-directional current.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 2, "A 9 V battery is connected across a single 45 Ω resistor. What current flows, in mA?", 200, "mA", "I = V/R = 9/45 = 0.200 A = 200 mA. (Watch the 10³ factor between A and mA.)", {"dg":1});
MS("c03", 2, "Which of these are scalar quantities? (Select all that apply.)", ["Displacement","Power","Force","Work","Acceleration"], [1,3], "Power and work have magnitude only. Displacement, force and acceleration are vectors (they have direction).", {"dg":1});
MC("c19", 2, "The thin region near a P–N junction that is swept free of mobile holes and electrons, and which holds the built-in potential barrier, is called the:", ["depletion region","forward-bias layer","intrinsic semiconductor","interbarrier"], 0, "Electrons diffuse across the junction and recombine with holes, leaving fixed ions and no mobile carriers: the depletion region.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c01", 4, "An electron lies on a straight line between two protons: one proton is 53 μm to its left and the other is 29 μm to its right. Find the magnitude of the net electrostatic force on the electron, in units of 10⁻¹⁹ N. (k = 8.99×10⁹ N·m²/C², e = 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C.)", 1.92, "×10⁻¹⁹ N", "Each proton attracts the electron toward itself, so the two forces point in opposite directions and subtract. F = k e² (1/r₂² − 1/r₁²) = (8.99×10⁹)(1.602×10⁻¹⁹)²(1/(29×10⁻⁶)² − 1/(53×10⁻⁶)²) ≈ 1.92×10⁻¹⁹ N, directed toward the nearer (29 μm) proton.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-dist"});
MC("c05", 3, "A 12 V battery drives the circuit shown: an 8 Ω resistor in series with a 3 Ω and a 5 Ω resistor in parallel, in series with a 6 Ω resistor. Which statement is TRUE?", ["The equivalent resistance is about 14.5 Ω.","The current at point G is greater than the current at point F.","The voltage drop between G and H equals the voltage drop between E and F.","The power dissipated by the 8 Ω resistor is 3.4 W."], 2, "Parallel branches always share the same voltage, so V_GH = V_EF (true). Req = 8 + (3∥5) + 6 = 15.875 Ω, not 14.5 Ω. The 5 Ω branch (point G) carries less current than the 3 Ω branch (point F). I = 12/15.875 = 0.7559 A, so P₈ = I²R = 4.57 W, not 3.4 W.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-q16"});
NUM("c05", 3, "In the circuit shown, a 12 V battery is in series with an 8 Ω resistor, then a parallel pair (3 Ω and 5 Ω), then a 6 Ω resistor. How much power (W) does the 8 Ω resistor dissipate?", 4.57, "W", "Req = 8 + 15/8 + 6 = 15.875 Ω. I = 12/15.875 = 0.7559 A. P = I²R = 0.7559² × 8 = 4.57 W.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-q16"});
NUM("c02", 4, "A 1000 μF capacitor has been connected through the switch to a 5.00 V battery (position a) for a very long time. At t = 0 the switch is flipped to position b, which connects the 47 kΩ resistor and capacitor in a loop to ground. What is the capacitor voltage (V) at t = 5.0 s?", 4.5, "V", "τ = RC = 47 000 × 1000×10⁻⁶ = 47 s. Discharging: V = V₀ e^(−t/τ) = 5.00 e^(−5/47) = 4.5 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-rc"});
NUM("c02", 4, "A 1000 μF capacitor is fully discharged and in series with a 47 kΩ resistor. At t = 0 the switch is thrown to position a, connecting the series RC to a 5.00 V battery. What is the capacitor voltage (V) at t = 47 s?", 3.16, "V", "τ = RC = 47 s, and t = τ exactly. Charging: V = V_b(1 − e^(−t/τ)) = 5.00 (1 − e⁻¹) = 3.16 V (63.2 % of 5 V).", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-rc"});
MC("c02", 4, "Two identical square-plate capacitors (parallel plates separated by a dielectric) are considered. The side length of each square plate is doubled. To what thickness of dielectric must the gap be changed to keep the capacitance the same?", ["Four times the original","Half the original","The same","One quarter of the original"], 0, "C = εA/d. Doubling the side length makes the area 4 times larger, so d must also become 4 times larger to keep C unchanged.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c10", 3, "A sinusoidal alternating current of 10.0 A (RMS) flows through a 25.0 Ω resistance. What is the peak voltage of this supply, in V?", 353.6, "V", "V_rms = I_rms R = 10.0 × 25.0 = 250 V. V_peak = √2 V_rms = 1.414 × 250 = 353.6 V.", {"dg":1});
MC("c01", 3, "Which statement about electric field lines is FALSE?", ["For a single isolated charge, the field lines start or end at a finite distance from the charge.","The number of field lines drawn is proportional to the magnitude of the charge.","The field is strong where the lines are close together and weak where they spread apart.","Field lines are perpendicular to the surface of a conductor."], 0, "Lines leave a positive charge and go out to infinity (or end on a negative charge); for a single charge they start at the charge and end at infinity, never at some finite distance. The other three statements are true.", {"dg":1});
MC("c10", 2, "In a standard North-American wall outlet, which wire(s) are connected to earth ground at the service panel?", ["Black (hot) and white (neutral)","White (neutral) and bare (ground)","Black (hot) and bare (ground)","Black (hot) only"], 1, "The neutral is bonded to earth at the main panel and the bare ground wire is connected to earth too. The black (hot) wire is at 120 V relative to them.", {"dg":1});
MC("c08", 3, "In the circuit shown, an independent voltage source v drives a series loop containing a resistor R and a diamond-shaped source labelled “a·v_x”, where v_x is the voltage across R (a is a constant). The diamond is what kind of component?", ["Voltage-controlled voltage source (VCVS)","Voltage-controlled current source (VCCS)","Current-controlled voltage source (CCVS)","Current-controlled current source (CCCS)"], 0, "A diamond denotes a dependent source. Its output is a voltage (+/− marks) and its control quantity is a voltage (v_x), so it is a VCVS.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-dep"});
NUM("c04", 3, "A four-band resistor is colour-coded Orange–Red–Black–Brown. It is connected in series with an ideal ammeter across a 9 V battery. What current (A) does the ammeter read?", 0.281, "A", "Orange = 3, red = 2, black = ×10⁰ (×1), brown = ±1 % tolerance, so R = 32 Ω. I = 9/32 = 0.281 A.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c33", 3, "A 5 V battery is connected as shown: R₁ = 4 Ω in series, then node N₁ from which R₂ (4 Ω) in series with R₃ (8 Ω) goes to the bottom rail; R₄ = 10 Ω joins N₁ to node N₂; at N₂, R₅ = 4 Ω goes to the bottom rail in parallel with R₆ (2 Ω) in series with R₇ (2 Ω). What is the equivalent resistance seen by the battery, in Ω?", 10, "Ω", "R₂+R₃ = 12 Ω. R₆+R₇ = 4 Ω, in parallel with R₅ (4 Ω) gives 2 Ω. R₄ + 2 = 12 Ω. 12 ∥ 12 = 6 Ω. Add R₁: 6 + 4 = 10 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-q27"});
NUM("c33", 4, "A 5 V battery is connected as shown: R₁ = 4 Ω in series, then node N₁ from which R₂ (4 Ω) in series with R₃ (8 Ω) goes to the bottom rail; R₄ = 10 Ω joins N₁ to node N₂; at N₂, R₅ = 4 Ω goes to the bottom rail in parallel with R₆ (2 Ω) in series with R₇ (2 Ω). Find the current through R₅, in A.", 0.125, "A", "Total current = 5/10 = 0.5 A. Drop across R₁ = 2 V, so V(N₁) = 3 V. Branch R₄–(2 Ω) carries 3/12 = 0.25 A, so V(N₂) = 0.25 × 2 = 0.5 V. I₅ = 0.5/4 = 0.125 A.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-q27"});
NUM("c33", 4, "A 5 V battery is connected as shown: R₁ = 4 Ω in series, then node N₁ from which R₂ (4 Ω) in series with R₃ (8 Ω) goes to the bottom rail; R₄ = 10 Ω joins N₁ to node N₂; at N₂, R₅ = 4 Ω goes to the bottom rail in parallel with R₆ (2 Ω) in series with R₇ (2 Ω). Find the voltage drop across R₄, in V.", 2.5, "V", "V(N₁) = 5 − 0.5×4 = 3 V. R₄ is in series with the 2 Ω equivalent of the right-hand group (12 Ω total), so I₄ = 3/12 = 0.25 A and V₄ = 0.25 × 10 = 2.5 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-q27"});
NUM("c33", 4, "A 5 V battery is connected as shown: R₁ = 4 Ω in series, then node N₁ from which R₂ (4 Ω) in series with R₃ (8 Ω) goes to the bottom rail; R₄ = 10 Ω joins N₁ to node N₂; at N₂, R₅ = 4 Ω goes to the bottom rail in parallel with R₆ (2 Ω) in series with R₇ (2 Ω). Find the power dissipated in R₃, in W.", 0.5, "W", "V(N₁) = 3 V across R₂+R₃ = 12 Ω gives I = 0.25 A. P₃ = I²R = 0.25² × 8 = 0.5 W.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-q27"});
MC("c21", 3, "Four 2-input NAND gates are wired as shown: gate 1 takes A and B; gate 2 takes A and the output of gate 1; gate 3 takes B and the output of gate 1; gate 4 takes the outputs of gates 2 and 3 and produces Q. Which single gate does the whole circuit behave like?", ["XOR","XNOR","AND","OR"], 0, "Let g = NAND(A,B). Then Q = NAND(NAND(A,g), NAND(B,g)). A=B=0 → g=1, both middle gates give 1, Q=0. A=1,B=0 → g=1, gate 2 = 0, gate 3 = 1, Q=1. A=B=1 → g=0, both middle gates give 1, Q=0. Q is 1 only when A ≠ B: XOR.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-nand"});
MC("c13", 2, "Which law gives the DIRECTION of the induced EMF and current in electromagnetic induction (the induced current opposes the change in flux that produces it)?", ["Faraday’s law (magnitude only)","Lenz’s law","Ampère’s law","Ohm’s law"], 1, "Faraday’s law gives the size of the induced EMF; the minus sign, Lenz’s law, says the induced current opposes the flux change.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c11", 4, "A proton (q = 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C) moves perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field at 2.5×10³ m/s and feels a magnetic force of 2.4×10⁻¹⁸ N. What is the field strength, in mT?", 5.99, "mT", "F = qvB for v ⊥ B, so B = F/(qv) = 2.4×10⁻¹⁸ / (1.602×10⁻¹⁹ × 2.5×10³) = 0.00599 T = 5.99 mT.", {"dg":1});
MC("c11", 4, "The weber (Wb), the SI unit of magnetic flux, equals 1 V·s. Expressed in base SI units it is:", ["kg·m²·s⁻²·A⁻¹","kg·m²·s⁻³·A⁻¹","kg·m·s⁻²·A⁻¹","kg·m²·s⁻²·A"], 0, "V = W/A = kg·m²·s⁻³·A⁻¹. Multiply by seconds: Wb = V·s = kg·m²·s⁻²·A⁻¹. (Check: Φ = B·A with B in kg·s⁻²·A⁻¹ and A in m².)", {"dg":1});
NUM("c08", 4, "A 10 V source with R₁ = 10 Ω feeds node A from the left; a 20 V source with R₂ = 20 Ω feeds node A from the right (both + terminals up). R₃ = 40 Ω connects node A to the bottom rail B and is the load. Remove the load R₃. Find the Thévenin voltage V_th seen at terminals A–B, in V.", 13.33, "V", "Nodal with the load removed: (V_A − 10)/10 + (V_A − 20)/20 = 0 → 2V_A − 20 + V_A − 20 = 0 → V_A = 40/3 = 13.33 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-thev"});
NUM("c08", 4, "A 10 V source with R₁ = 10 Ω feeds node A from the left; a 20 V source with R₂ = 20 Ω feeds node A from the right (both + terminals up). R₃ = 40 Ω connects node A to the bottom rail B and is the load. Find the Thévenin resistance R_th seen at A–B with the load removed and both voltage sources replaced by shorts, in Ω.", 6.667, "Ω", "With the sources shorted, R₁ and R₂ are in parallel: 10 × 20 / 30 = 6.67 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-thev"});
NUM("c08", 4, "A 10 V source with R₁ = 10 Ω feeds node A from the left; a 20 V source with R₂ = 20 Ω feeds node A from the right (both + terminals up). R₃ = 40 Ω connects node A to the bottom rail B and is the load. Find the Norton current I_N (the short-circuit current from A to B), in A.", 2, "A", "I_N = V_th / R_th = 13.33/6.667 = 2 A. (Check: 10/10 + 20/20 = 2 A.)", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-thev"});
NUM("c08", 4, "A 10 V source with R₁ = 10 Ω feeds node A from the left; a 20 V source with R₂ = 20 Ω feeds node A from the right (both + terminals up). R₃ = 40 Ω connects node A to the bottom rail B and is the load. Using a Thévenin equivalent, find the current through the load R₃ = 40 Ω, in A.", 0.286, "A", "I = V_th/(R_th + R₃) = 13.33/(6.667 + 40) = 0.2857 A.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-thev"});
NUM("c08", 4, "In the bridge circuit shown, a 10 V source feeds two dividers: left arm R₁ = 1.50 kΩ (top) and R₂ = 808 Ω (bottom); right arm R₃ = 907 Ω (top) and R_x = 174 Ω (bottom). V_out is measured from the left mid-point (+) to the right mid-point (−). What is V_out, in V?", 1.891, "V", "Left mid-point = 10 × 808/(1500+808) = 3.501 V. Right mid-point = 10 × 174/(907+174) = 1.61 V. V_out = 3.501 − 1.61 = 1.891 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-bridge"});
NUM("c20", 3, "The ideal op-amp shown has +5 V on its non-inverting (+) input. A 220 Ω resistor connects the inverting (−) input to ground and a 570 Ω feedback resistor connects the output to the inverting input. What is V_out, in V? (Assume the supply rails are large enough.)", 17.95, "V", "Non-inverting amplifier: V_out = V_in (1 + R_f/R_g) = 5 (1 + 570/220) = 5 × 3.591 = 17.95 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-opamp"});
NUM("c02", 4, "Find the equivalent capacitance between terminals a and b, in μF. The network is: two 5 μF capacitors in parallel, in series with the parallel combination of (i) three capacitors 2 μF, 6 μF and 3 μF in series and (ii) two 3 μF capacitors in parallel, which are in series with a 6 μF capacitor.", 2.857, "μF", "Two 5 μF in parallel = 10 μF. Series stack: 1/C = 1/2 + 1/6 + 1/3 = 1 → 1 μF. Two 3 μF in parallel = 6 μF, in series with 6 μF → 3 μF. Those two branches are in parallel: 1 + 3 = 4 μF. Total: 10 and 4 in series → 40/14 = 2.857 μF.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-cap"});
MC("c22", 3, "A student wants to find the resistance of a lamp in a circuit that is connected to a battery with the switch closed and the lamp glowing. She sets her multimeter to the ohmmeter function and touches the probes across the lamp. Is this a correct way to measure the lamp’s resistance?", ["No. The ohmmeter supplies its own test current, so the component must be removed or the circuit de-energised, otherwise the reading is wrong and the meter can be damaged.","Yes. An ohmmeter reads the resistance of any component that has voltage across it.","Yes, but only if the probes are swapped to red-on-negative.","No. Resistance can only be found by reading voltage and current together with an ammeter in parallel."], 0, "An ohmmeter injects a small known current and measures the voltage it produces; it assumes no other source. A live circuit adds voltage and parallel paths, corrupting the reading and possibly destroying the meter. Turn the circuit off and isolate the part (or use V and I with Ohm’s law).", {"dg":1});
MC("c03", 3, "The siemens (S) is the SI unit of conductance and susceptance. How is it related to the basic circuit units?", ["S = 1/Ω = A/V (the reciprocal of the ohm)","S = Ω·A (the ohm times the ampere)","S = V/A (the same as the ohm)","S = V·A (the same as the watt)"], 0, "Conductance G = 1/R, so 1 S = 1 Ω⁻¹ = 1 A/V. (Formerly called the mho.)", {"dg":1});
MC("c13", 3, "Most homes use AC rather than DC mainly because:", ["AC can be stepped up by transformers to a very high voltage for efficient long-distance transmission and stepped down again to a safe voltage for homes","AC is always safer to touch than DC at the same voltage","AC requires thicker wires, which makes transmission cheaper","Transformers work on DC too, but AC motors never need them"], 0, "Power loss in the lines is I²R, so sending the same power at a higher voltage (lower current) wastes far less. Transformers only work with changing flux, so they need AC. Transmission at hundreds of kV is stepped down to 120/240 V near homes.", {"dg":1});
MC("c11", 3, "What is the difference between a temporary magnet and a permanent magnet?", ["A temporary magnet (soft iron) is magnetised only while it is in an external field or a current flows, and loses it soon after; a permanent magnet (hard material) retains its magnetisation","A temporary magnet has only one pole; a permanent magnet has two","A temporary magnet is always weaker than a permanent magnet of the same size; otherwise they are identical","A temporary magnet repels iron; a permanent magnet attracts it"], 0, "Soft magnetic materials (soft iron) have low coercivity: their domains re-randomise when the field is removed. Hard materials (steel, NdFeB, alnico) keep their domain alignment. Every magnet has two poles.", {"dg":1});
MC("c14", 3, "Which of these is NOT one of the four main types of DC motor?", ["Induction-wound","Series-wound","Shunt-wound","Compound-wound"], 0, "The main types are series (field in series with armature), shunt (field in parallel), compound (both) and permanent-magnet. “Induction” motors are AC machines.", {"dg":1});
MC("c14", 3, "In a shunt-wound DC motor, how is the field winding connected relative to the armature?", ["In parallel with the armature","In series with the armature","Both in series and in parallel (two windings)","Not connected to the supply at all"], 0, "Shunt = field winding in parallel with the armature (high-resistance, many turns), giving nearly constant speed. Series = field in series with armature (high starting torque). Compound has both.", {"dg":1});
MC("c19", 3, "Which diode type is deliberately operated in reverse breakdown to hold a nearly constant voltage?", ["Zener diode","Schottky diode","LED","Standard rectifier diode"], 0, "A Zener is heavily doped so that it breaks down at a precise reverse voltage (V_Z) and then holds that voltage over a wide current range. A Schottky has a metal–semiconductor junction with a low forward drop (~0.2–0.3 V) and fast switching.", {"dg":1});
MS("c19", 4, "Which statements about diodes are correct? (Select all that apply.)", ["A Schottky diode has a lower forward voltage drop than a silicon PN diode.","An LED emits light when it is forward biased.","A Zener diode’s I–V curve has a nearly vertical section in reverse bias at V_Z.","An avalanche diode works in forward bias only."], [0,1,2], "Schottky: ~0.2–0.3 V vs 0.7 V for silicon. LED: photons are released by carrier recombination under forward bias. Zener: the breakdown region is almost vertical at V_Z. Avalanche diodes are designed to break down in reverse bias (by avalanche multiplication), so the last statement is false.", {"dg":1});
MC("c15", 3, "A switch with one input contact that can connect to either of two output contacts is called:", ["SPDT (single-pole, double-throw)","DPST (double-pole, single-throw)","SPST (single-pole, single-throw)","DPDT (double-pole, double-throw)"], 0, "Pole = number of separate circuits switched; throw = number of positions each pole can connect to. One pole with two throw positions is SPDT. SPST is a simple on/off; DPST is two on/offs ganged; DPDT is two SPDTs ganged (e.g. motor reversing).", {"dg":1});
MC("c10", 4, "A neon indicator bulb is connected to a source of static electricity (DC) and only ONE of its two electrodes glows, while on AC both glow alternately. Which statement is correct?", ["On DC the glow forms at the negative electrode (cathode); on AC the two electrodes alternate as cathode, so both glow","On DC the glow forms at the positive electrode (anode)","Both electrodes always glow but one is too faint to see on DC","The glow depends on the gas pressure, not on polarity"], 0, "The negative glow surrounds the cathode, where electrons leave the metal and excite the neon. On AC each electrode is the cathode for half of each cycle, so both appear lit.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c33", 4, "Five identical 2 Ω resistors are arranged as follows: resistor R_a in series with a parallel combination of resistor R_b and a series pair made of R_c and a parallel pair (R_d ∥ R_e). What is the equivalent resistance of the arrangement, in Ω?", 3.2, "Ω", "R_d ∥ R_e = 1 Ω. R_c + 1 = 3 Ω. R_b ∥ 3 Ω = 2×3/5 = 1.2 Ω. Add R_a: 2 + 1.2 = 3.2 Ω. (Puzzle use: this is one of the few 5-resistor arrangements that gives 3.2 Ω.)", {"dg":1});
NUM("c33", 5, "An infinite ladder network: each “rung” consists of a 10 Ω resistor in series along the top rail, followed by a 10 Ω resistor from top to bottom rail (the pattern repeats forever). What is the equivalent resistance between the left-hand terminals A and D, in Ω?", 16.18, "Ω", "Adding one more section does not change an infinite ladder, so R_eq = R + (R·R_eq)/(R + R_eq). With R = 10: R_eq² − 10R_eq − 100 = 0, R_eq = 10(1+√5)/2 = 16.18 Ω (the golden ratio times R).", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-ladder"});
NUM("c33", 5, "A 10 V battery is connected across a bridge network: top-left R₁ = 12 Ω, top-right R₂ = 18 Ω, bottom-left R₄ = 18 Ω, bottom-right R₅ = 12 Ω, and a middle resistor R₃ = 6 Ω joining the left and right mid-points. Find the equivalent resistance seen by the battery, in Ω.", 14.57, "Ω", "Unbalanced bridge (12/18 ≠ 18/12), so use nodal analysis with the bottom node at 0 V and the top at 10 V. Node a (left): (a−10)/12 + a/18 + (a−b)/6 = 0. Node b (right): (b−10)/18 + b/12 + (b−a)/6 = 0. Solving: a = 5.294 V, b = 4.706 V. Battery current = (10−a)/12 + (10−b)/18 = 0.6863 A, so R_eq = 10/0.6863 = 14.57 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-br5"});
NUM("c33", 5, "Twelve identical 1 Ω resistors form the edges of a cube. Current flows in at one corner (A) and out at the diagonally opposite corner (B). What is the equivalent resistance between A and B, in Ω?", 0.8333, "Ω", "By symmetry, the three neighbours of A are at the same potential, as are the three neighbours of B. So: 3 parallel 1 Ω edges (1/3 Ω), then 6 parallel middle edges (1/6 Ω), then 3 parallel edges (1/3 Ω). Total = 1/3 + 1/6 + 1/3 = 5/6 Ω = 0.833 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-cube"});
MC("c11", 4, "A proton moving to the right (toward +x) enters a region of uniform magnetic field pointing INTO the page. What is the direction of the magnetic force on the proton?", ["Up the page (+y)","Down the page (−y)","Into the page","To the left (opposite to v)"], 0, "F = qv × B. With v = +x̂ and B = −ẑ (into the page): x̂ × (−ẑ) = +ŷ. A positive charge feels a force up the page. (A negative charge would go down.)", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-proton"});
NUM("c11", 4, "A proton enters a uniform magnetic field with velocity v perpendicular to B and feels a force F. An alpha particle (charge 3.2×10⁻¹⁹ C) enters the same field at the same velocity, also perpendicular to B. By what factor is the force on the alpha particle larger than F?", 2, "×F", "F = qvB, so the force is proportional to charge. q_alpha/q_proton = 3.2×10⁻¹⁹/1.6×10⁻¹⁹ = 2, so F_alpha = 2F (same direction, since both are positive).", {"dg":1});
MC("c11", 3, "A long straight wire carries a current flowing straight UP this page (toward the top edge). Looking from the top of the page down along the wire, which way does the magnetic field circulate around it?", ["Counter-clockwise (right-hand rule: thumb along I, fingers curl along B)","Clockwise","Radially outward","Parallel to the wire in the direction of I"], 0, "Right-hand rule: point your thumb along the current (toward the viewer); your fingers curl counter-clockwise. The field forms closed circles around the wire and never points along it.", {"dg":1});
MC("c21", 2, "Which of these is a universal logic gate (any other gate can be built from copies of it alone)?", ["NAND","XOR","AND","OR"], 0, "NAND and NOR are universal. For example NOT = NAND(A,A), AND = NOT(NAND(A,B)), OR = NAND(NOT A, NOT B).", {"dg":1});
MC("c21", 3, "A circuit has a NOT gate on input A feeding one input of an OR gate, whose other input is the output of an AND gate with inputs B and C. The output is X. What is the Boolean expression for X?", ["X = A′ + B·C","X = A′ · (B + C)","X = A + B·C′","X = (A·B)′ + C"], 0, "NOT gives A′. AND gives B·C. The OR combines them: X = A′ + BC.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-logic"});
NUM("c21", 3, "A circuit has a NOT gate on input A feeding one input of an OR gate, whose other input is the output of an AND gate with inputs B and C. The output is X. Out of the 8 possible input combinations (A, B, C), for how many is X = 1?", 5, "of 8", "X = A′ + BC. When A = 0 (4 combinations) X = 1 regardless. When A = 1 (4 combinations) X = BC, which is 1 only for B = C = 1 (1 combination). Total = 4 + 1 = 5.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-t3-logic"});
NUM("c33", 4, "In the circuit shown a 24 V battery drives a 6 Ω resistor in series with a parallel pair (R_x and 12 Ω) and a 4 Ω resistor. The battery current is to be exactly 2.00 A. Find R_x in Ω.", 2.4, "Ω", "Total resistance = 24/2 = 12 Ω. Subtract the series parts: 12 − 6 − 4 = 2 Ω for the parallel pair. R_x ∥ 12 = 2 → 1/R_x = 1/2 − 1/12 = 5/12 → R_x = 2.4 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-unkR"});
NUM("c33", 5, "A 24 V source is connected directly to node N₁ of the ladder shown: 2 kΩ from each of N₁, N₂, N₃ to ground, 1 kΩ from N₁ to N₂ and 1 kΩ from N₂ to N₃, plus a final 2 kΩ at the far right also connected to N₃ and ground. Find the voltage at N₃, in V.", 6, "V", "Look from the right: at N₃ the two 2 kΩ resistors in parallel = 1 kΩ. Looking into the 1 kΩ + 1 kΩ from N₂ gives 2 kΩ, which in parallel with the 2 kΩ at N₂ is 1 kΩ. So each 1 kΩ series resistor meets an equal 1 kΩ to ground: V drops by half at each node. V(N₂) = 12 V, V(N₃) = 6 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-r2r"});
NUM("c33", 5, "In the ladder shown, a 24 V source is connected directly to node N₁. There are 2 kΩ resistors from N₁, N₂, N₃ to ground and another 2 kΩ from N₃ to ground at the far end; 1 kΩ resistors join N₁–N₂ and N₂–N₃. What current does the 24 V source supply, in mA?", 24, "mA", "At N₁, the 2 kΩ to ground is in parallel with (1 kΩ + 1 kΩ) = 2 kΩ (as seen from N₂ onward: 1 kΩ in series with the equivalent of N₂ onward, which is 1 kΩ). So R_in = 2 ∥ 2 = 1 kΩ and I = 24 V / 1 kΩ = 24 mA.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-r2r"});
NUM("c33", 5, "Six identical 12 Ω resistors join four nodes X, Y, Z, W so that every pair of nodes is connected by exactly one resistor. What is the equivalent resistance between X and Y, in Ω?", 6, "Ω", "By symmetry Z and W are at the same potential, so the resistor between them carries no current and can be removed. What remains between X and Y: the direct 12 Ω, the path X–Z–Y (24 Ω) and the path X–W–Y (24 Ω), all in parallel: 1/R = 1/12 + 1/24 + 1/24 = 1/6, so R = 6 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-tetra"});
NUM("c33", 5, "Twelve identical 12 Ω resistors form the edges of a cube. Current enters at one corner and leaves at the ADJACENT corner (the other end of one edge). What is the equivalent resistance between those two corners, in Ω?", 7, "Ω", "Use Foster’s theorem: for any resistor network, the sum over all resistors of (effective resistance between its ends)/(its resistance) equals n − 1, where n is the number of nodes. Here n = 8 and all 12 edges are equivalent by symmetry, so each edge has R_eff = (7/12) R. The two corners are the ends of one edge: R_eff = 7 × 12 Ω/12 = 7 Ω. (The direct 12 Ω edge in parallel with the rest of the cube, 12 ∥ 16.8 Ω = 7 Ω, agrees.)", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-cube-adj"});
NUM("c34", 4, "In the three-node network shown, a 12 V battery feeds node N₁ through 2 Ω. Resistors: N₁–ground 6 Ω, N₁–N₂ 3 Ω, N₂–ground 6 Ω, N₂–N₃ 6 Ω, N₃–ground 3 Ω. A 1 A current source pushes current into N₃ (from ground). Find the voltage at N₃, in V.", 3.556, "V", "Write KCL at each node (conductances in S): N₁: (V₁−12)/2 + V₁/6 + (V₁−V₂)/3 = 0; N₂: (V₂−V₁)/3 + V₂/6 + (V₂−V₃)/6 = 0; N₃: (V₃−V₂)/6 + V₃/3 = 1. Solving gives V₁ = 7.556 V, V₂ = 4.667 V, V₃ = 3.556 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-nodal3"});
NUM("c35", 4, "A 12 V battery feeds node X through R₁ = 3 Ω. R₂ = 6 Ω connects X to ground. A 3 A ideal current source pushes current into X from ground. Using superposition or nodal analysis, find V_X, in V.", 14, "V", "Superposition: battery alone gives 12 × 6/9 = 8 V. Current source alone: 3 A into (3 ∥ 6 = 2 Ω) gives 6 V. Total = 14 V. (Nodal check: (V−12)/3 + V/6 = 3 → V = 14 V.)", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-super"});
MC("c35", 5, "A 12 V battery feeds node X through R₁ = 3 Ω. R₂ = 6 Ω connects X to ground, and a 3 A ideal current source pushes current into X from ground. What is the battery doing?", ["Absorbing (being charged with) about 8 W","Delivering about 8 W","Delivering 36 W","Delivering nothing; it carries no current"], 0, "V_X = 14 V > 12 V, so current flows from X back through R₁ into the battery: I = (14−12)/3 = 0.667 A entering the + terminal. P = 12 × 0.667 = 8 W absorbed.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-super"});
NUM("c08", 5, "A 12 V source drives a bridge. Left arm: R₁ = 6 Ω (top), R₂ = 3 Ω (bottom). Right arm: R₃ = 4 Ω (top), R₄ = 8 Ω (bottom). Terminals P and Q are the left and right mid-points. Find the magnitude of the open-circuit voltage between P and Q (the Thévenin voltage), in V.", 4, "V", "V_P = 12 × 3/(6+3) = 4 V. V_Q = 12 × 8/(4+8) = 8 V. |V_PQ| = 4 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-thevbr"});
NUM("c08", 5, "A 12 V source drives a bridge. Left arm: R₁ = 6 Ω (top), R₂ = 3 Ω (bottom). Right arm: R₃ = 4 Ω (top), R₄ = 8 Ω (bottom). Terminals P and Q are the left and right mid-points. Find the Thévenin resistance seen at P–Q, in Ω.", 4.667, "Ω", "Short the source. From P: R₁ ∥ R₂ = 6∥3 = 2 Ω. From Q: R₃ ∥ R₄ = 4∥8 = 2.667 Ω. They are in series between P and Q: R_th = 4.667 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-thevbr"});
NUM("c08", 5, "A 12 V source drives a bridge. Left arm: R₁ = 6 Ω (top), R₂ = 3 Ω (bottom). Right arm: R₃ = 4 Ω (top), R₄ = 8 Ω (bottom). A variable load resistor R_L is connected between P and Q and adjusted for maximum power transfer. What maximum power (W) does it dissipate?", 0.857, "W", "V_th = |4 − 8| = 4 V, R_th = 2 + 2.667 = 4.667 Ω. Maximum power is delivered when R_L = R_th; P_max = V_th²/(4R_th) = 16/(4 × 4.667) = 0.857 W.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-thevbr"});
NUM("c02", 4, "At t = 0 the switch closes, connecting a 12 V source through 6 kΩ to a node that has a 3 kΩ resistor and an initially uncharged 100 μF capacitor in parallel to ground. What is the capacitor voltage at t = 0.30 s, in V?", 3.107, "V", "Thévenin seen by the capacitor: V_th = 12 × 3/9 = 4 V, R_th = 6 ∥ 3 = 2 kΩ, τ = 2000 × 100×10⁻⁶ = 0.20 s. V(0.30) = 4 (1 − e^(−1.5)) = 3.107 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-rc2"});
NUM("c02", 5, "In the circuit shown (12 V source, switch S, 6 kΩ in series; then 3 kΩ and 100 μF in parallel to ground) the capacitor starts uncharged. S is closed for 0.40 s and then opened. What is the capacitor voltage 0.30 s after S opens, in V?", 1.272, "V", "Closed: τ₁ = (6 ∥ 3) kΩ × 100 μF = 0.20 s, V_final = 4 V, so V(0.40) = 4(1 − e⁻²) = 3.459 V. Open: the capacitor discharges through the 3 kΩ only, τ₂ = 3000 × 100×10⁻⁶ = 0.30 s. V = 3.459 × e⁻¹ = 1.272 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-rc2"});
NUM("c02", 4, "A 6 μF capacitor C₁ is charged to 12 V and then disconnected from the battery. The switch is closed, connecting it in parallel with an uncharged 3 μF capacitor C₂ (plates of the same polarity joined). What is the final voltage across the pair, in V?", 8, "V", "Charge is conserved: Q = 6 μF × 12 V = 72 μC. The parallel combination is 9 μF, so V = 72/9 = 8 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-share"});
NUM("c02", 5, "A 6 μF capacitor C₁ is charged to 12 V and disconnected from the battery; it is then connected in parallel with an uncharged 3 μF capacitor C₂. How much energy is lost (converted to heat and radiation) in the process, in μJ?", 144, "μJ", "Initial energy = ½(6 μF)(12 V)² = 432 μJ. Final: V = 8 V, C = 9 μF, U = ½(9 μF)(8 V)² = 288 μJ. Lost = 143.99999999999997 μJ (one third of the original, independent of the wire resistance).", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-share"});
NUM("c02", 4, "A 2 μF and a 3 μF capacitor are connected in series across a 30 V battery and charge fully. What is the voltage across the 3 μF capacitor, in V?", 12, "V", "Series capacitors carry the same charge: C_eq = 6/5 = 1.2 μF, Q = 1.2 × 30 = 36 μC. V₃ = Q/C = 36/3 = 12 V (the smaller capacitor gets the larger voltage).", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-seriescap"});
MC("c02", 5, "A 2 μF and a 3 μF capacitor are charged in series from a 30 V battery. The battery is then removed and the capacitors are separated from each other and reconnected in parallel with OPPOSITE polarity: the positive plate of each is joined to the negative plate of the other. What is the final voltage across the parallel combination?", ["0 V","6.0 V","7.2 V","14.4 V"], 0, "In series each capacitor holds Q = 36 μC. Opposite-polarity parallel connection: net charge = 36 − 36 = 0, so the final voltage is 0 V. All the stored energy (540 μJ) is dissipated. (Same-polarity parallel would give 72/5 = 14.4 V.)", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-seriescap"});
NUM("c20", 4, "Two ideal op-amp stages are in cascade. Stage 1 is inverting with a 10 kΩ input resistor and a 50 kΩ feedback resistor. Stage 2 is non-inverting (input on the + pin) with 10 kΩ from the − pin to ground and a 30 kΩ feedback resistor. V_in = 0.3 V. Find V_out, in V.", -6, "V", "Stage 1 gain = −50/10 = −5, giving −1.5 V. Stage 2 gain = 1 + 30/10 = 4, giving −6 V. Overall gain = −20 → V_out = 0.3 × (−20) = −6 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-oacasc"});
NUM("c20", 5, "A summing amplifier has inputs +2.0 V through 10 kΩ and +1.0 V through 20 kΩ into the inverting node, a 40 kΩ feedback resistor, and ±9 V supply rails. Assume the output can swing all the way to the rails. What is V_out, in V?", -9, "V", "Ideal result: V_out = −R_f (V₁/R₁ + V₂/R₂) = −40 k (0.2 mA + 0.05 mA) = −10 V. But the output cannot go below the −9 V rail, so the op-amp saturates at about −9 V (and the “virtual short” no longer holds).", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-oasum"});
NUM("c20", 5, "A summing amplifier has inputs +2.0 V through 10 kΩ and V₂ through 20 kΩ into the inverting node, and a 40 kΩ feedback resistor. The supply is ±15 V. What value of V₂ (in V) makes V_out = −6 V?", -1, "V", "V_out = −40 k (2/10 k + V₂/20 k) = −6 → 0.2 mA + V₂/20 kΩ = 0.15 mA → V₂ = −1.0 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-oasum"});
NUM("c19", 4, "In the diode clipper shown, a 10 V-peak sine wave drives a 1 kΩ resistor; the output node has a diode (anode to the output) in series with a 3 V battery (+ terminal toward the diode) to ground. The diode drop is 0.7 V and the output is unloaded. What is the peak-to-peak output voltage, in V?", 13.7, "V", "The diode conducts only when V_out > 3 + 0.7 = 3.7 V, so the positive peaks are clipped at +3.7 V. On the negative half-cycle the diode is reverse biased and the output follows the input down to −10 V. V_pp = 3.7 − (−10) = 13.7 V.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-clip"});
NUM("c19", 4, "An NPN transistor switches a 240 Ω relay coil from a 12 V supply (ignore V_CE(sat)). The base is driven from a 5 V logic signal through a resistor R_B (V_BE = 0.7 V). To saturate the transistor firmly, the designer chooses a forced gain of 10 (I_B = I_C/10). Find R_B, in Ω.", 860, "Ω", "I_C = 12/240 = 50 mA. I_B = 5 mA. R_B = (5 − 0.7)/0.005 = 860 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-bjt"});
NUM("c18", 4, "Nine white LEDs (3.2 V forward drop at their rated 20 mA) are powered from a 12 V supply as three identical parallel strings, each with three LEDs in series and one series resistor. Find the resistor value per string, in Ω.", 119.99999999999993, "Ω", "Each string: three LEDs drop 9.6 V, leaving 12 − 9.6 = 2.4 V across the resistor at 20 mA: R = 2.4/0.02 = 120 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-led"});
NUM("c18", 4, "Nine white LEDs (3.2 V at their rated 20 mA) are powered from a 12 V supply as three identical parallel strings of three LEDs in series, each with its own series resistor sized so that each LED carries exactly 20 mA. What percentage of the power drawn from the 12 V supply is dissipated in the LEDs themselves?", 80, "%", "Supply current = 3 × 20 mA = 60 mA, so P_supply = 12 × 0.06 = 0.72 W. LEDs: 9 × 3.2 × 0.02 = 0.576 W. Efficiency = 0.576/0.72 = 80 % (the rest is lost in the 120 Ω resistors).", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-led"});
MC("c18", 4, "You want to light LEDs (forward voltage 3.2 V at 20 mA) from a 12 V supply with one current-limiting resistor per string. What is the maximum number of LEDs you can put in one series string?", ["3","4","5","2"], 0, "Three LEDs need 9.6 V, leaving 2.4 V for the resistor. Four LEDs would need 12.8 V > 12 V, so they would never reach full current (they would barely glow or not light). The resistor needs some voltage left over to set the current.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c21", 4, "Inputs A, B, C. Gate 1 is a NAND of A and B. Gate 2 is an OR of B and C. Gate 3 is an AND of the outputs of gates 1 and 2, giving Y. For how many of the 8 input combinations is Y = 1?", 4, "of 8", "Y = (AB)′ · (B + C). Enumerate: Y = 1 needs AB ≠ 11 and (B or C). B=0: need C=1 → (A,B,C) = 001, 101 (2 cases). B=1: need A=0 → 010, 011 (2 cases; A=1,B=1 gives NAND = 0). Total 4.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-logic3"});
MC("c21", 4, "What is the minimum number of 2-input NAND gates needed to build a 2-input OR gate?", ["3","2","4","1"], 0, "A + B = (A′ · B′)′ = NAND(NOT A, NOT B). Each NOT is a NAND with its inputs tied together (2 gates), and a third NAND combines them. Total 3. (AND needs 2, NOT needs 1, XOR needs 4.)", {"dg":1});
MC("c21", 5, "A staircase light is controlled by three switches A, B, C (one at the bottom, one at the top, one in the middle) so that flipping ANY single switch always changes the light from on to off or off to on. Which Boolean expression describes the light?", ["L = A ⊕ B ⊕ C (odd parity: on when an odd number of switches are up)","L = A·B·C","L = A + B + C","L = A·B + B·C + A·C (majority)"], 0, "The light must toggle whenever one input toggles, which is exactly what XOR (parity) does: L = A ⊕ B ⊕ C. AND, OR and majority do not always change when one input changes. (In practice this needs two 3-way switches and an intermediate/4-way switch wiring.)", {"dg":1});
NUM("c23", 4, "A voltmeter with 1 MΩ input resistance is connected across the lower 500 kΩ resistor of a divider made of two 500 kΩ resistors in series across 12 V. What does the meter read, in V?", 4.8, "V", "Without the meter the reading would be 6.0 V. The meter (1 MΩ) in parallel with 500 kΩ gives 333.3 kΩ. V = 12 × 333.3/(500 + 333.3) = 4.8 V: a 20 % loading error.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-loadv"});
NUM("c23", 4, "A 50 Ω resistor is in series with a 5 V source, so the true current without any meter is 100 mA. An ammeter whose internal resistance is 10 Ω is inserted in series. What does it read, in mA?", 83.33, "mA", "Total resistance = 50 + 10 = 60 Ω, so I = 5/60 = 83.3 mA. The meter’s burden reduces the current by 16.7 %. An ideal ammeter has zero resistance.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-amm"});
NUM("c03", 4, "A copper wire has a resistance of 8.0 Ω. It is melted and drawn into a wire three times as long (the same volume of copper, uniform cross-section, same temperature). What is its new resistance, in Ω?", 72, "Ω", "R = ρL/A. The volume L·A is fixed, so tripling L cuts A to one third. R becomes 3 × 3 = 9 times larger: 8 × 9 = 72 Ω.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 4, "A uniform wire has resistance 12 Ω. It is cut into two equal halves and the two halves are connected side by side (in parallel). What is the resistance of the combination, in Ω?", 3, "Ω", "Each half is 6 Ω (half the length). Two 6 Ω resistors in parallel give 3 Ω — one quarter of the original.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 3, "A 50 m length of copper wire (resistivity 1.68×10⁻⁸ Ω·m) has a diameter of 1.0 mm. What is its resistance, in Ω?", 1.07, "Ω", "A = π(0.5×10⁻³)² = 7.85×10⁻⁷ m². R = ρL/A = 1.68×10⁻⁸ × 50 / 7.85×10⁻⁷ = 1.07 Ω.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 4, "A wire of length L and diameter d has resistance R. A second wire of the same metal has length 2L and diameter 2d. Express its resistance as a multiple of R.", 0.5, "×R", "R ∝ L/A ∝ L/d². Doubling L doubles R; doubling d makes the area 4 times larger and divides R by 4. Net: 2/4 = 0.5 R.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 3, "A 1500 W space heater runs 6 hours a day for 30 days. Electricity costs $0.15 per kWh. What is the cost, in dollars?", 40.5, "$", "Energy = 1.5 kW × 6 h × 30 = 270 kWh. Cost = 270 × 0.15 = $40.50.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 4, "A family replaces ten 60 W incandescent bulbs with ten 9 W LED bulbs. Each bulb is on 5 hours per day, every day. Electricity costs $0.16 per kWh. How many dollars are saved per year (365 days)?", 148.92, "$", "Power saved per bulb = 51 W; total = 510 W = 0.51 kW. Energy = 0.51 × 5 × 365 = 930.75 kWh. Savings = 930.75 × 0.16 = $148.92.", {"dg":1});
MC("c05", 4, "A 60 W / 120 V bulb and a 100 W / 120 V bulb are connected in SERIES across a 120 V supply. Which statement is correct?", ["The 60 W bulb glows brighter, since it has the higher resistance and dissipates more power (I²R)","The 100 W bulb glows brighter because it is the higher-power bulb","Both glow equally because the same current flows through both","Both glow at their rated power since 120 V is applied"], 0, "R₆₀ = 120²/60 = 240 Ω, R₁₀₀ = 120²/100 = 144 Ω. In series the same current flows, so P = I²R favours the larger R: the 60 W bulb. I = 120/384 = 0.3125 A, P₆₀ = 23.4 W, P₁₀₀ = 14.1 W. Both are dimmer than rated.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-2bulb"});
NUM("c05", 4, "A 60 W / 120 V bulb and a 100 W / 120 V bulb (treat their resistances as constant) are connected in series across a 120 V supply. How much power does the 60 W bulb actually dissipate, in W?", 23.4, "W", "R₆₀ = 240 Ω, R₁₀₀ = 144 Ω, I = 120/384 = 0.3125 A. P₆₀ = I²R = 23.4 W.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-2bulb"});
NUM("c05", 4, "Three identical lamps (resistance R each) are connected as shown: lamp A in series with the parallel combination of lamp B and lamp C, where C is in series with a switch S. Initially S is open. S is then closed. By what factor does the TOTAL power drawn from the battery change (P_after / P_before)?", 1.333, "×", "Before: only A and B carry current, R_total = 2R, P = V²/2R. After: B ∥ C = R/2, R_total = 1.5R, P = V²/1.5R. At fixed V the power ratio is R_before/R_after = 2R/1.5R = 4/3 = 1.333.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-3bulb"});
MC("c05", 5, "Three identical lamps (resistance R each) are connected as shown: lamp A in series with the parallel pair of lamp B and lamp C (C in series with switch S). Initially S is open, then S is closed. What happens to the brightness of lamps A and B?", ["A gets brighter (by a factor 16/9), B gets dimmer (to 4/9 of its former power)","A gets brighter, B also gets brighter","A gets dimmer, B gets brighter","Neither changes because the battery voltage is constant"], 0, "Before: I = V/2R, P_A = P_B = V²/4R. After: I_A = V/1.5R = 2V/3R, P_A = (4/9)V²/R (= 16/9 of before); B and C each carry I_A/2 = V/3R so P_B = V²/9R = 4/9 of before. The extra parallel path lowers the total resistance, so A gets more current, while the voltage across the B∥C pair falls.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-3bulb"});
NUM("c05", 4, "Three identical lamps A, B, C are in series across a battery. Closing the switch S shorts out lamp C. By what factor does the power dissipated in lamp A increase (P_after/P_before)? (Treat the lamp resistance as constant.)", 2.25, "×", "Before: I = V/3R. After: I = V/2R. P = I²R, so the ratio = (1/2)²/(1/3)² = 9/4 = 2.25.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-3ser"});
NUM("c07", 4, "Two batteries feed a three-resistor network: 18 V (left) with R₁ = 3 Ω, 6 V (right) with R₃ = 2 Ω, and R₂ = 6 Ω connecting the junction of R₁ and R₃ to the bottom rail. All + terminals are at the top. Find the current through R₂, in A.", 1.5, "A", "Nodal at the junction V: (V−18)/3 + (V−6)/2 + V/6 = 0 → multiply by 6: 2V − 36 + 3V − 18 + V = 0 → V = 9 V. I₂ = 9/6 = 1.5 A. (Mesh check: I₁ = 3 A, I₃ = 1.5 A flowing into the 6 V source.)", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-mesh"});
MC("c07", 5, "Two batteries feed a three-resistor network: 18 V (left) with R₁ = 3 Ω, 6 V (right) with R₃ = 2 Ω, and R₂ = 6 Ω from the junction of R₁ and R₃ to the bottom rail. All + terminals are at the top. What does the 6 V battery do?", ["It absorbs about 9 W (it is being charged)","It delivers about 9 W","It delivers about 18 W","It absorbs about 3 W"], 0, "The junction is at 9 V, which is higher than 6 V, so current (9−6)/2 = 1.5 A flows into the + terminal of the 6 V battery. It absorbs P = 6 × 1.5 = 9 W.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-mesh"});
NUM("c08", 4, "A series loop contains a 12 V battery, a 4 Ω resistor, a 2 Ω resistor and a current-controlled voltage source whose voltage is (3 Ω)·I, where I is the loop current. The dependent source is oriented so that it opposes the battery (it absorbs voltage). Find I, in A.", 1.333, "A", "KVL: 12 − 4I − 2I − 3I = 0 → I = 12/9 = 1.333 A. A dependent source that depends on the loop current behaves like a 3 Ω resistor here.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-ccvs"});
NUM("c08", 4, "In the Wheatstone bridge shown (24 V source; top-left R₁ = 120 Ω, bottom-left R₂ = 180 Ω, top-right R₃ = 200 Ω, bottom-right R_x), the galvanometer reads zero. Find R_x, in Ω.", 300, "Ω", "Balance: R₁/R₂ = R₃/R_x → R_x = R₃ R₂/R₁ = 200 × 180/120 = 300 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-wheat"});
NUM("c08", 4, "The Wheatstone bridge shown is balanced (24 V source; R₁ = 120 Ω and R₂ = 180 Ω on the left, R₃ = 200 Ω and R_x on the right). What current does the battery supply, in A?", 0.128, "A", "Balanced → R_x = 300 Ω and no current in the galvanometer. Left arm: 120 + 180 = 300 Ω. Right arm: 200 + 300 = 500 Ω. Parallel: 187.5 Ω. I = 24/187.5 = 0.128 A.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-wheat"});
NUM("c05", 4, "A 12 V supply drives a load that needs exactly 5 V at 10 mA through a divider: R₁ in series from the supply, then the load in parallel with R₂ = 500 Ω to ground. Find R₁, in Ω.", 350, "Ω", "R₂ carries 5 V/500 Ω = 10 mA. R₁ carries load + R₂ = 20 mA and must drop 12 − 5 = 7 V: R₁ = 7/0.02 = 350 Ω.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-divdes"});
NUM("c05", 3, "A 10 kΩ potentiometer across a 10 V supply is set to mid-travel (5 kΩ above the wiper, 5 kΩ below). The wiper feeds a 10 kΩ load to ground. What voltage appears at the wiper, in V?", 4, "V", "The load (10 kΩ) is in parallel with the lower 5 kΩ: 3.33 kΩ. V = 10 × 3.33/(5 + 3.33) = 4 V — not 5 V, because of loading.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-pot"});
NUM("c03", 4, "A 100 Ω and a 400 Ω resistor, each rated ¼ W, are connected in SERIES. What is the largest supply voltage that can be applied without exceeding either rating, in V?", 12.5, "V", "Current limits: 100 Ω: √(0.25/100) = 50 mA; 400 Ω: √(0.25/400) = 25 mA. The smaller (25 mA) governs. V = 25 mA × 500 Ω = 12.5 V.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c03", 5, "A 100 Ω and a 400 Ω resistor, each rated ¼ W, are connected in PARALLEL. What is the maximum total power the pair can dissipate without exceeding either rating, in W?", 0.3125, "W", "Voltage limits: 100 Ω: √(0.25×100) = 5 V; 400 Ω: √(0.25×400) = 10 V. The smaller (5 V) governs. P = 5²/100 + 5²/400 = 0.25 + 0.0625 = 0.3125 W.", {"dg":1});
MS("c36", 4, "Using all three resistors 10 Ω, 20 Ω and 30 Ω exactly once, in any series/parallel arrangement, which of these equivalent resistances can be obtained? (Select all that apply.)", ["13.33 Ω","18 Ω","27.5 Ω","45 Ω"], [0,2], "Achievable values: 60, 5.45 (all parallel), 22 (10 + 20∥30), 27.5 (20 + 10∥30), 36.67 (30 + 10∥20), 8.33 (10 ∥ 50), 13.33 (20 ∥ 40), 15 (30 ∥ 30). So 13.33 and 27.5 are possible; 18 and 45 are not. Sanity rule: series-parallel results lie between the parallel total (5.45) and the series total (60).", {"dg":1});
NUM("c19", 4, "A Zener regulator: a 12 V supply, a 220 Ω series resistor, and a 5.6 V Zener diode in parallel with a 1 kΩ load. Find the current through the Zener, in mA.", 23.5, "mA", "Current through the series resistor = (12 − 5.6)/220 = 29.09 mA. The load takes 5.6/1000 = 5.6 mA. The Zener carries the rest: 23.5 mA.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-zener"});
NUM("c19", 5, "A Zener regulator: a 12 V supply, a 220 Ω series resistor, and a 5.6 V Zener diode in parallel with a load. The load is changed to 100 Ω. What is the output voltage, in V?", 3.75, "V", "Check whether the Zener conducts: without it, V_out = 12 × 100/(220 + 100) = 3.75 V, which is below 5.6 V, so the Zener stays off (no breakdown) and the output is 3.75 V: the regulator has dropped out of regulation.", {"dg":1,"fig":"qf-c1-h-zener"});
NUM("c06", 4, "A battery has an open-circuit voltage of 12.6 V. When it supplies a 4.0 A load its terminal voltage falls to 11.8 V. What is its internal resistance, in Ω?", 0.2, "Ω", "The lost voltage is 12.6 − 11.8 = 0.8 V = I r → r = 0.8/4 = 0.20 Ω.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c06", 5, "A battery has an open-circuit voltage of 12.6 V and an internal resistance of 0.20 Ω. What is the maximum power it can deliver to an external load resistor, in W?", 198.5, "W", "Maximum power transfer occurs when R_load = r = 0.20 Ω. Then I = 12.6/0.4 = 31.5 A and P = I²R = 31.5² × 0.2 = 198.5 W (only half of the total is useful; the other half heats the battery).", {"dg":1});
NUM("c02", 4, "A 47 μF capacitor charges through a 10 kΩ resistor from a fixed DC source. How long does it take to reach 90 % of the source voltage, in seconds?", 1.08, "s", "τ = RC = 10 000 × 47×10⁻⁶ = 0.47 s. 1 − e^(−t/τ) = 0.9 → t = τ ln 10 = 0.47 × 2.303 = 1.08 s.", {"dg":1});
MC("c02", 5, "A 1000 μF capacitor is charged from empty to 5.00 V through a resistor from an ideal 5.00 V battery. How much energy does the RESISTOR dissipate as heat during the charge, and how does it depend on the resistance value?", ["12.5 mJ, independent of the resistance","25 mJ, independent of the resistance","12.5 mJ for a large R but less for a small R","0 mJ, since an ideal capacitor dissipates no energy"], 0, "The battery supplies Q V = C V² = 25 mJ. The capacitor stores ½CV² = 12.5 mJ. The other 12.5 mJ is dissipated in the resistor regardless of R (a large R just takes longer).", {"dg":1});
NUM("c10", 4, "A sine wave of 10 V peak is half-wave rectified (the negative half-cycles are removed, ideal diode). What is the RMS voltage of the output, in V?", 5, "V", "Mean square = ½ × (V_p²/2) (half of the time the signal is zero) = V_p²/4, so V_rms = V_p/2 = 5 V. (A full sine would give 7.07 V.)", {"dg":1});
MC("c25", 3, "Four identical lamps are connected in series across a 12 V battery and none lights. A technician measures 0 V across lamps 1, 2 and 4, and 12 V across lamp 3. What is the fault?", ["Lamp 3 has a broken filament (open circuit)","Lamp 3 is shorted","The battery is dead","Lamp 1 is open"], 0, "With an open in a series circuit no current flows, so there is no drop across the good lamps (0 V), and the full supply voltage appears across the open element (12 V). A shorted lamp would read 0 V.", {"dg":1});
MC("c25", 4, "Three 30 Ω resistors are wired in parallel across a 6 V supply. One of them burns out open. What is the new equivalent resistance, and what happens to the current in the other two?", ["15 Ω; their currents are unchanged","45 Ω; their currents drop by half","15 Ω; their currents double","10 Ω; their currents are unchanged"], 0, "Two 30 Ω in parallel = 15 Ω (before: 10 Ω). Each parallel branch still sees the full 6 V, so each still carries 0.2 A; only the total current (0.4 A instead of 0.6 A) changes.", {"dg":1});
MC("c22", 3, "A student sets a multimeter to the current (A) range and connects the probes directly across a 9 V battery. What is most likely to happen?", ["A very large current flows through the meter: its fuse blows (or the battery and meter are damaged)","The meter shows the battery voltage","The meter shows 0 A because there is no resistor","The meter shows infinite resistance"], 0, "A meter on its ammeter range has almost zero resistance (a shunt). Connecting it across a voltage source creates a near short circuit; the current is limited only by the battery and leads, and the meter fuse should blow.", {"dg":1});
MC("c16", 4, "You build a circuit from the schematic of a 3 V battery with one resistor, one LED and one switch all in a single loop. You install the LED backwards. With the switch closed, what happens?", ["The LED stays dark and almost no current flows, because the LED is reverse biased","The LED lights dimly because the resistor limits the current","The LED is destroyed at once by the 3 V","The LED lights normally because it is in series"], 0, "An LED conducts only when forward biased. Reversed, it blocks (leakage only, nanoamps), so the series circuit carries almost no current. At only 3 V the reverse voltage is well below a typical LED’s reverse rating (about 5 V), so no damage occurs: the LED just stays dark.", {"dg":1});
MS("c16", 4, "Which of these circuit-symbol/meter connection statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)", ["A voltmeter is connected in parallel with the element being measured.","An ammeter is connected in series with the element being measured.","A fuse symbol is placed in parallel with the load to protect it.","The ohmmeter function should be used only on an unpowered component."], [0,1,3], "Voltmeters go across (parallel) because they have high resistance; ammeters go in series because they have low resistance; ohmmeters supply their own current, so the circuit must be off. A fuse protects by being in series with the load; in parallel it would do nothing useful (or short the circuit when it blows).", {"dg":1});
NUM("c36", 4, "A quick sanity check: a 9 V battery is connected to three resistors, 1 kΩ, 2 kΩ and 3 kΩ, all in SERIES. The voltage across the 3 kΩ resistor is computed to be how many volts? (Compute it exactly.)", 4.5, "V", "V₃ = 9 × 3/(1+2+3) = 4.5 V. Sanity check: the largest resistor gets the largest share (half of the total here), and the three voltages (1.5 + 3 + 4.5) add to 9 V.", {"dg":1});
NUM("c09", 4, "A car battery has an EMF of 12 V and an internal resistance of 0.020 Ω. A steel wrench with a resistance of 0.010 Ω is accidentally laid across its terminals. How much power does the wrench dissipate, in W?", 1600, "W", "Total resistance = 0.020 + 0.010 = 0.030 Ω, so I = 12/0.030 = 400 A. P_wrench = I²R = 400² × 0.010 = 1600 W: the wrench glows red-hot. This is why metal tools near battery terminals are a burn and fire hazard, even though 12 V itself cannot push a dangerous current through dry skin.", {"dg":1});
MC("c09", 4, "Why can you safely touch both terminals of a 12 V car battery with dry hands, yet a wrench across them is dangerous?", ["Dry skin has a very high resistance (tens of kΩ or more) so the current is well under 1 mA, whereas the wrench is nearly a short circuit that draws hundreds of amps","12 V is too low to drive any current at all through the body, but a wrench has a lower voltage rating","Skin is a perfect insulator, and the wrench is a perfect conductor, so only the wrench conducts current","The wrench stores charge like a capacitor and releases it as a spark"], 0, "I = V/R: for R_skin ≈ 100 kΩ, I ≈ 0.12 mA (imperceptible). For a wrench, R ≈ 0.01 Ω, so I is hundreds of amps, limited only by the battery’s internal resistance. Hazard depends on current, not just voltage.", {"dg":1});
